  {
    "document": {
      "PatspecterEmbedding": [],
      "doi": "10.1002/9783527853205.ch22",
      "publicationDate": "2025-04-04",
      "title": "Hydroformylation",
      "type": "other",
      "url": "https://openalex.org/works/W4409187898",
      "abstract": "This chapter explains hydroformylation, which is one of the most important homogeneously catalysed reactions, and discusses catalyst metals, ligands and mechanisms in hydroformylation. In hydroformylation, the main reaction involves the addition of a hydrogen atom and a formyl group to an alkene. Aldehydes, especially the linear ones, are of great importance to the chemical industry. The chapter illustrates the most frequently used ligand types in hydroformylation. In one industrial hydroformylation process, a watersoluble ligand, triphenylphosphinetrisulfonate sodium salt is used. To date, the industrial applications of hydroformylation are dominated by propene as a substrate. During recent decades, a host of developments have been made in industrial hydroformylation processes. The reactivity of alkenes in hydroformylation reactions follows the usual trend, also seen in hydrogenation and other hydrofunctionalisation reactions. The asymmetric hydroformylation is attracting increasing attention for the synthesis of pharmaceuticals and fine chemicals.",
